<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tamil Nadu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tamil Nadu is a state of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coromandel-coast">Coromandel Coast</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a>. It formed the core of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamilakam">Tamilakam</a>, ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Cheras</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a>, and is known for its great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples and classical Tamil literature. The state took its present borders as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madras-state">Madras State</a> in 1956 on linguistic lines and was renamed Tamil Nadu in 1969.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu.jpg" alt="Tamil Nadu" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: pnglife (Flickr) · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mamallapuram_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a first period of power in the early medieval centuries, they were conquered by the Chola Empire in the tenth century and spent most of the eleventh and twelfth centuries as subordinate or rebellious princes in the far south of <b>Tamil Nadu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Later Pandyan Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They emerged as the Satavahana dynasty and its successor in the east, the Ikshvaku dynasty, declined, and their heartland was Tondaimandalam, the country around Kanchipuram in the north of present-day <b>Tamil Nadu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Travancore takes its name from Thiruvithamcode, in the present Kanyakumari district of <b>Tamil Nadu</b>, contracted to Thiruvankode and anglicised by the English.</blockquote><a href="/empire/travancore">Travancore · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Vijayanagara architecture, with its tall gateway towers, pillared halls carved with horses and mythical beasts and large temple-city complexes, set a style followed across the south, and many temples in <b>Tamil Nadu</b> and Andhra Pradesh were rebuilt or enlarged under its rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the south, Jainism flourished in Karnataka and <b>Tamil Nadu</b> in the first millennium CE under dynasties such as the Western Gangas, whose minister Chavundaraya had the great statue of Bahubali carved at Shravanabelagola around 983; Jain monks contributed greatly to early literature in Tamil and Kannada.</blockquote><a href="/religion/jainism">Jainism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Tamil is the main language of most people in <b>Tamil Nadu</b> and Puducherry in India and in the Northern and Eastern Provinces of Sri Lanka, and small minorities speak it in other Indian states and in Colombo and the Sri Lankan hill country.</blockquote><a href="/language/tamil">Tamil · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
